# Biome enforces LF. Without this, a checkout on Windows rewrites every file to CRLF and the
# formatter fails on code nobody touched.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.pdf binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary

pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true

# docs/reference redistributes official AEAT, BOE and W3C documents **byte for byte**, and
# MANIFEST.md publishes a SHA-256 per file so anyone can verify that without trusting us.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above broke exactly that: git rewrote the CRLF line endings of
# SistemaFacturacion.wsdl, ConsultaLR.xsd and RespuestaConsultaLR.xsd to LF on commit. The files
# on a Windows working tree stayed as the AEAT published them, so the manifest matched locally
# and failed on the Linux runner — where the checkout gets the altered bytes.
#
# `-text` means "store and check out these bytes unchanged, on every platform". It is not a
# formatting preference here: the AEAT's reuse terms forbid «la desnaturalización del contenido»,
# and a hash that only matches on one operating system proves nothing.
docs/reference/** -text linguist-vendored=true
